4. OPEN-SOURCE COMPONENTS AND THE BITBYBIT OCCT KERNEL

The Runner incorporates Open-Source Components that are licensed under their
respective open-source licenses (including MIT and Apache 2.0).
Nothing in this License restricts your rights under those open-source
licenses with respect to those components. A complete list of Open-Source
Components and their licenses is provided in the accompanying NOTICE file.

Bitbybit OCCT Kernel: the Bitbybit OCCT Kernel contains Open CASCADE
Technology (OCCT), licensed under LGPL v2.1 with the Open CASCADE exception,
statically linked with the Bitbybit kernel layer of MB "Bit by bit
developers". It is licensed under the MIT License, as the @bitbybit-dev/occt
npm package states, not under this License; it is closed source, and the
source code of the kernel layer is not published. The NOTICE file describes
how to obtain the materials to relink the kernel with a modified OCCT. You
may use a kernel relinked that way with the Runner, by putting it, and a Web
Worker that works with the JavaScript glue its link produced, in place of the
originals under the same file names in your self-hosted copy of the CDN
Assets. For that purpose, and for your own use, you may modify the Runner's
OCCT Web Worker files so that they work with such a relinked kernel, and
reverse-engineer them to the extent necessary to debug such modifications;
sections 3(a) and 3(b) do not restrict this. Nothing in this License
restricts what the MIT License of the Bitbybit OCCT Kernel or the license of
OCCT permits.

If you wish to modify or use the Open-Source Components independently of
the Runner, the NOTICE file says where the source code of each is
available; the Bitbybit open-source libraries are at
https://github.com/bitbybit-dev/bitbybit

